task: adaptive-one-producer
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-07
branch: feature/adaptive-one-producer
worktree: ../okay-wt-alloc

why: the last thing between `Channel.apply` and a buffer that never
  slows down when producers arrive. `Queues.strong.adaptive` beats the
  ring at four producers (135 vs 725), sixteen (99 vs 2375) and with
  several consumers (1039 vs 2307), and loses only at ONE producer,
  by about 1.2x. Three causes were tried and refuted (solo-part): the
  part lookup, the consumer-side thread-local, and the extra layer of
  call. What the evidence points at is ALLOCATION: 1 446 KB/op against
  the ring's 780 at the same work, with method-handle frames under
  receiveManyAsync in the adaptive lane and nowhere else.

what: an allocation profile that NAMES THE TYPE before any code
  changes — JFR's ObjectAllocationSample on
  `ManyProducersBenchmark.adaptive_chunk` and `oneRing_chunk` at
  producers=1, diffed. Then fix what it names, measure with the
  minimum-of-five protocol, and either land the number or record the
  refutation.

predict: a per-element or per-chunk closure or box that exists only on
  the partitioned path — most likely the `sink` function of
  `popMany(max)(sink)` failing to scalar-replace once the call is not
  inlined, or a `Chunk`/`ChunkBuf` copy the adaptive path takes and
  the ring does not.
  Disqualifying: the two lanes allocate the SAME types in the same
  proportion, which would mean the 1.2x is not allocation at all and
  the gap needs a cycles profile (perfasm) instead.
